ic compass</li><li>they remembered where stars rose and set by making up detailed <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">35</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q35\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>when it was cloudy, they found the direction by using <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">36</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q36\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li></ul><p><b>− Finding new islands</b></p><ul><li>they could identify certain <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">37</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q37\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> that only live near land</li><li>close to land, they could read changes in the sea’s <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">38</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q38\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li></ul><p><b>− Recent history</b></p><ul><li>in 1967 the canoe Hokule’a sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti without <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">39</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q39\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>now replica traditional canoes have sailed across the Pacific and around the world</li><li>as well as sailing, these voyages have created fresh interest in Polynesian culture, music and <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">40</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-trainer-2-t4-l-q40\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"source-questions\"><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div cl